ML_PAGE("l4-prep", "<header class=\"page-header theme-l4\"><span class=\"eyebrow\">LECTURE 4 · MCQ PREPARATION</span><h2>Lecture 4 MCQ Prep</h2><p>The complete MCQ preparation notes in their original order: 50 multiple-choice questions and 7 coding questions sit inside the sections they belong to, with answers hidden until you reveal them.</p></header><div class=\"study-grid three-col\"><div class=\"metric-card\"><strong>50</strong><span>MCQs in the notes</span></div><div class=\"metric-card\"><strong>7</strong><span>Coding questions</span></div><div class=\"metric-card\"><strong>WHY</strong><span>Explain why each distractor is wrong</span></div></div><div class=\"action-row\"><button class=\"primary-btn bg-l4\" data-quiz=\"4\">Start Lecture 4 simulator</button></div><details class=\"topic-index toc-panel theme-l4\" id=\"l4p-toc\"><summary><strong>MCQ notes</strong><span class=\"toc-count\">30 sections</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><input type=\"search\" class=\"toc-search\" placeholder=\"Filter sections...\" aria-label=\"Filter sections\"><ul class=\"toc-list\"><li data-full=\"Lecture 4 — MCQ Exam Preparation\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4p-s0\" title=\"Lecture 4 — MCQ Exam Preparation\">Overview</button></li><li data-full=\"21. What you should know instantly\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4p-s1\" title=\"21. What you should know instantly\"><span class=\"toc-no\">21</span>Key facts table</button></li><li class=\"toc-group\">PART A — TEXT</li><li data-full=\"22. Text preprocessing\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4p-s3\" title=\"22. Text preprocessing\"><span class=\"toc-no\">22</span>Text preprocessing</button></li><li data-full=\"23. Bag of Words\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4p-s4\" title=\"23. Bag of Words\"><span class=\"toc-no\">23</span>Bag of Words</button></li><li data-full=\"24. N-grams\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4p-s5\" title=\"24. N-grams\"><span class=\"toc-no\">24</span>N-grams</button></li><li data-full=\"25. TF-IDF\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4p-s6\" title=\"25. TF-IDF\"><span class=\"toc-no\">25</span>TF-IDF</button></li><li data-full=\"26. Cosine similarity\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4p-s7\" title=\"26. Cosine similarity\"><span class=\"toc-no\">26</span>Cosine similarity</button></li><li data-full=\"27. Document clustering\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4p-s8\" title=\"27. Document clustering\"><span class=\"toc-no\">27</span>Document clustering</button></li><li data-full=\"28. Topic modelling / LDA\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4p-s9\" title=\"28. Topic modelling / LDA\"><span class=\"toc-no\">28</span>Topic modeling (LDA)</button></li><li data-full=\"29. Word embeddings\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4p-s10\" title=\"29. Word embeddings\"><span class=\"toc-no\">29</span>Word embeddings</button></li><li data-full=\"30. CBOW vs Skip-gram\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4p-s11\" title=\"30. CBOW vs Skip-gram\"><span class=\"toc-no\">30</span>CBOW vs skip-gram</button></li><li class=\"toc-group\">PART B — TEMPORAL FEATURES</li><li data-full=\"31. Extracting dates\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4p-s13\" title=\"31. Extracting dates\"><span class=\"toc-no\">31</span>Date parts</button></li><li data-full=\"32. Time-of-day binning\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4p-s14\" title=\"32. Time-of-day binning\"><span class=\"toc-no\">32</span>Time-of-day bins</button></li><li data-full=\"33. Time zones\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4p-s15\" title=\"33. Time zones\"><span class=\"toc-no\">33</span>Time zones</button></li><li data-full=\"34. Epoch\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4p-s16\" title=\"34. Epoch\"><span class=\"toc-no\">34</span>Epoch time</button></li><li class=\"toc-group\">PART C — IMAGE FEATURES</li><li data-full=\"35. RGB representation\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4p-s18\" title=\"35. RGB representation\"><span class=\"toc-no\">35</span>RGB channels</button></li><li data-full=\"36. Grayscale\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4p-s19\" title=\"36. Grayscale\"><span class=\"toc-no\">36</span>Grayscale</button></li><li data-full=\"37. Flattening\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4p-s20\" title=\"37. Flattening\"><span class=\"toc-no\">37</span>Flattening pixels</button></li><li data-full=\"38. Intensity histograms\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4p-s21\" title=\"38. Intensity histograms\"><span class=\"toc-no\">38</span>Intensity histograms</button></li><li data-full=\"39. Statistical image features\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4p-s22\" title=\"39. Statistical image features\"><span class=\"toc-no\">39</span>Image statistics</button></li><li data-full=\"40. Canny edge detection\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4p-s23\" title=\"40. Canny edge detection\"><span class=\"toc-no\">40</span>Canny edges</button></li><li data-full=\"41. HOG\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4p-s24\" title=\"41. HOG\"><span class=\"toc-no\">41</span>HOG</button></li><li data-full=\"42. SURF\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4p-s25\" title=\"42. SURF\"><span class=\"toc-no\">42</span>SURF</button></li><li data-full=\"43. Visual Bag of Words\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4p-s26\" title=\"43. Visual Bag of Words\"><span class=\"toc-no\">43</span>Visual Bag of Words</button></li><li data-full=\"44. CNNs\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4p-s27\" title=\"44. CNNs\"><span class=\"toc-no\">44</span>CNNs</button></li><li class=\"toc-group\">PART D — INTEGRATION, CODING &amp; REVIEW</li><li data-full=\"45. Lecture 4 coding questions\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4p-s29\" title=\"45. Lecture 4 coding questions\"><span class=\"toc-no\">45</span>Coding questions</button></li><li data-full=\"46. Integration / pipeline MCQs\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4p-s30\" title=\"46. Integration / pipeline MCQs\"><span class=\"toc-no\">46</span>Pipeline order</button></li><li data-full=\"47. Lecture 4 answer key\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4p-s31\" title=\"47. Lecture 4 answer key\"><span class=\"toc-no\">47</span>Answer key</button></li><li data-full=\"48. The distinctions I would memorize hardest for Lecture 4\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4p-s32\" title=\"48. The distinctions I would memorize hardest for Lecture 4\"><span class=\"toc-no\">48</span>Key distinctions</button></li><li data-full=\"49. Final rapid-fire test for both lectures\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4p-s33\" title=\"49. Final rapid-fire test for both lectures\"><span class=\"toc-no\">49</span>Rapid-fire review</button></li></ul></details><details class=\"prep-notes theme-l4\" open><summary><div><strong>Lecture 4 MCQ preparation</strong><small>Know-cold tables, traps, practice questions and coding questions — section by section, answers hidden.</small></div><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"markdown-body compact\"><div class=\"notes-toolbar\"><button type=\"button\" data-sections=\"open\">Expand all</button><button type=\"button\" data-sections=\"close\">Collapse all</button><span>30 sections · click a heading to open it</span></div><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4p-s0\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Lecture 4 — MCQ Exam Preparation</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><h4>Feature Engineering: Text, Temporal &amp; Image Data</h4>\n<p>Lecture 4 is conceptually different.</p>\n<p>Lecture 3 asked:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>How do we transform numbers and categories?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Lecture 4 asks:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>How do we convert complicated things like sentences, timestamps and images into numerical representations?</strong></p>\n</blockquote></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4p-s1\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">21. What you should know instantly</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Topic</th>\n<th>Immediate meaning</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Tokenization</td>\n<td>Split text into tokens/words</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Lowercasing</td>\n<td>Normalize capitalization</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Stopword removal</td>\n<td>Remove frequent low-information words</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Stemming</td>\n<td>Heuristic reduction to root</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Lemmatization</td>\n<td>Linguistically informed canonical form</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Bag of Words</td>\n<td>Word-count vectors</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>N-grams</td>\n<td>Contiguous sequences of $n$ words</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>TF</td>\n<td>Frequency in current document</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IDF</td>\n<td>Rarity across documents</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>TF-IDF</td>\n<td>TF × IDF</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cosine similarity</td>\n<td>Compare vector direction</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cosine ≈ 1</td>\n<td>Very similar</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cosine ≈ 0</td>\n<td>Weak/no similarity</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Document clustering</td>\n<td>Group similar texts without labels</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>LDA</td>\n<td>Discover latent topics</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Embeddings</td>\n<td>Dense semantic word vectors</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>CBOW</td>\n<td>Context → word</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Skip-gram</td>\n<td>Word → context</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Date extraction</td>\n<td>Year/month/day/etc.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Time binning</td>\n<td>Hour → Morning/Afternoon/etc.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>UTC normalization</td>\n<td>Put time zones on same reference</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Epoch</td>\n<td>Seconds since 1 Jan 1970 UTC</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>RGB</td>\n<td>Image represented by R/G/B channels</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Grayscale</td>\n<td>One intensity channel</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Flattening</td>\n<td>Matrix → 1D vector</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Histogram</td>\n<td>Intensity-frequency representation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Canny</td>\n<td>Edge detection</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>HOG</td>\n<td>Edge-orientation descriptor</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>SURF</td>\n<td>Local feature descriptors</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>VBOW</td>\n<td>Visual descriptors → visual word counts</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>CNN</td>\n<td>Automated feature learning</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div></details><h2 class=\"notes-divider\" id=\"l4p-d2\">PART A — TEXT</h2><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4p-s3\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">22. Text preprocessing</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-widget\" data-vis=\"l4_text_pipeline\"><div class=\"vis-title\">Text preprocessing pipeline</div><div class=\"vis-root\"></div><figcaption>Interactive · Type any sentence and watch each preprocessing step.</figcaption></figure></div><p>The lecture's pipeline includes tokenization, lowercasing, removing special characters, stopwords and stemming/lemmatization.</p>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q1</span><div><p>What does tokenization do?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Scales numbers</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Splits text into units such as words</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Detects image edges</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Calculates cosine similarity</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q2</span><div><p>Why lowercase text?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> So <code>\"Machine\"</code> and <code>\"machine\"</code> can be treated consistently</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> To remove every noun</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> To scale numbers</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> To create timestamps</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q3</span><div><p>Which is a stopword-like term?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> compressor_failure</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> bearing</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> the</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> turbine</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q4</span><div><p>Stemming and lemmatization both broadly attempt to:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Transform related word forms toward a common form</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Turn words into timestamps</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Detect outliers</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Create image pixels</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong></div></details></div></details></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4p-s4\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">23. Bag of Words</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q5</span><div><p>Bag of Words primarily represents text using:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> RGB values</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Word occurrence counts</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Time differences</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Category hashing only</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong></div></details></div></details>\n<p>Suppose vocabulary order is:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">cat, dog, sky, blue\n</code></pre>\n<p>Document:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">&quot;blue sky&quot;\n</code></pre>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q6</span><div><p>Which vector matches?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> <code>[1,1,0,0]</code></div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> <code>[0,0,1,1]</code></div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> <code>[0,1,0,1]</code></div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> <code>[1,0,1,0]</code></div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>The lecture defines BoW as a term-frequency vector over the corpus vocabulary.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q7</span><div><p>What is a major limitation of Bag of Words?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> It cannot represent numbers</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> It ignores word positions, syntax and semantic meaning</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> It requires image data</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> It cannot count repeated words</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong></div></details></div></details></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4p-s5\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">24. N-grams</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Given:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">machine learning model\n</code></pre>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q8</span><div><p>Which are the bigrams?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> <code>machine</code>, <code>learning</code>, <code>model</code></div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> <code>machine learning</code>, <code>learning model</code></div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> <code>machine learning model</code> only</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> <code>machine model</code></div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q9</span><div><p>Why can bigrams help with:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">not good\n</code></pre></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> They can preserve the phrase as a joint feature</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> They remove the word <code>not</code></div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> They convert text into images</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> They make every word identical</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong></div></details></div></details></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4p-s6\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">25. TF-IDF</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-widget\" data-vis=\"l4_tfidf\"><div class=\"vis-title\">BoW, TF-IDF and cosine similarity</div><div class=\"vis-root\"></div><figcaption>Interactive · Edit the documents; Bag-of-Words counts, TF-IDF weights and cosine similarities update live.</figcaption></figure></div><p>The intuition:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{TF} =\n\\text{importance/frequency within this document}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{IDF} =\n\\text{rarity across all documents}$$</div>\n<p>The lecture's core idea is:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>common-everywhere terms get penalized; distinctive terms get greater weight.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q10</span><div><p>A word appears in every document.</p>\n<p>Its IDF should generally be:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> High</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Low</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Infinite</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Random</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q11</span><div><p>A rare term appears in only a few documents.</p>\n<p>Its IDF tends to be:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Higher</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Zero</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Always negative</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Equal to term frequency</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q12</span><div><p>The lecture gives:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{TFIDF}=\\text{TF}\\times \\text{IDF}$$</div>\n<p>True or False?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> True</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> False</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong></div></details></div></details></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4p-s7\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">26. Cosine similarity</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-widget\" data-vis=\"l4_cosine\"><div class=\"vis-title\">Cosine similarity</div><div class=\"vis-root\"></div><figcaption>Interactive · Cosine similarity measures the angle between document vectors, not their length.</figcaption></figure></div><p>The lecture gives:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\cos(\\theta)\n=\n\\frac{A\\cdot B}\n{\\|A\\|\\|B\\|}$$</div>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q13</span><div><p>If cosine similarity is close to:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$1$$</div>\n<p>the documents are:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Usually very similar in vector direction</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Completely unrelated</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Duplicates by definition</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Missing data</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q14</span><div><p>If cosine similarity is near:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$0$$</div>\n<p>the documents are:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Highly aligned</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Relatively dissimilar</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Always identical</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Ordinal</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong></div></details></div></details></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4p-s8\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">27. Document clustering</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q15</span><div><p>A set of unlabeled documents is converted to TF-IDF vectors and automatically grouped into similar topics.</p>\n<p>This is:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Regression</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Unsupervised clustering</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Supervised classification</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Reinforcement learning</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>The lecture demonstrates weather documents clustering separately from animal documents.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4p-s9\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">28. Topic modelling / LDA</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q16</span><div><p>What does LDA attempt to discover?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Missing values</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Latent topics/themes in documents</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> RGB channels</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Numeric outliers</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q17</span><div><p>In topic modelling, a document may contain:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Exactly one word</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> A mixture of multiple topics</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Only one mandatory topic</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> No numeric representation</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong></div></details></div></details></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4p-s10\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">29. Word embeddings</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q18</span><div><p>Which best describes word embeddings?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Sparse one-hot vectors only</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Dense numerical vectors where semantically related words can be nearby</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Timestamps for words</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Image pixels</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q19</span><div><p>Why might vectors for <code>\"king\"</code> and <code>\"queen\"</code> be closer than <code>\"king\"</code> and <code>\"car\"</code>?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> They occur in more similar linguistic contexts</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> They have the same spelling</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> They always have the same length</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> They are one-hot encoded</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong></div></details></div></details></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4p-s11\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">30. CBOW vs Skip-gram</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q20</span><div><p>CBOW roughly predicts:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Context from target word</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Target word from context</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> RGB from grayscale</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Topic from timestamp</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q21</span><div><p>Skip-gram roughly predicts:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Context from target word</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Target word from context</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Income from age</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Categories from quantiles</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>The lecture explicitly contrasts CBOW and Skip-gram this way.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details><h2 class=\"notes-divider\" id=\"l4p-d12\">PART B — TEMPORAL FEATURES</h2><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4p-s13\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">31. Extracting dates</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-widget\" data-vis=\"l4_timestamp\"><div class=\"vis-title\">Timestamp feature extraction</div><div class=\"vis-root\"></div><figcaption>Interactive · One timestamp becomes many features — and the same instant looks different in each time zone.</figcaption></figure></div><p>Suppose:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">2026-09-23 14:35:22\n</code></pre>\n<p>You can derive:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">year\nmonth\nday\nquarter\nday_of_week\nhour\n</code></pre>\n<p>The lecture explicitly lists these kinds of features.</p>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q22</span><div><p>Extracting <code>month</code> from a timestamp is:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Feature engineering</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Data deletion</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Topic modelling</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Image segmentation</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q23</span><div><p>Why can extracted time components be better than the raw timestamp?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> They can expose patterns such as weekend, season or time-of-day</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Models cannot process any numeric values</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> They always reduce rows</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> They remove categories</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong></div></details></div></details></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4p-s14\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">32. Time-of-day binning</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Suppose:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">14:35\n</code></pre>\n<p>becomes:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Afternoon\n</code></pre>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q24</span><div><p>This combines:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Time extraction + binning</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> TF-IDF + hashing</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> CNN + clustering</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Dummy coding + stemming</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong></div></details></div></details></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4p-s15\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">33. Time zones</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q25</span><div><p>Malaysia:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">14:00 UTC+8\n</code></pre>\n<p>London:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">06:00 UTC+0\n</code></pre>\n<p>These may represent:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Completely different days only</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The same moment in time</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Missing data</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Image coordinates</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q26</span><div><p>Why normalize timestamps to UTC?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> To make global timestamps share a consistent temporal reference</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> To perform one-hot encoding</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> To eliminate all seasons</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> To create TF-IDF vectors</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong></div></details></div></details></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4p-s16\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">34. Epoch</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q27</span><div><p>Unix Epoch time represents:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Days since year 1</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Seconds since 1 January 1970 UTC</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Months since 1900</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Hours since model training</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q28</span><div><p>Why can epoch/ordinal representations be useful?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Easier elapsed-time calculations</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> They automatically classify observations</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> They remove images</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> They eliminate the need for timestamps</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong></div></details></div></details></div></details><h2 class=\"notes-divider\" id=\"l4p-d17\">PART C — IMAGE FEATURES</h2><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4p-s18\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">35. RGB representation</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q29</span><div><p>An RGB image generally has shape:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$(m,n,c)$$</div>\n<p>where $c$ usually equals:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 1</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 2</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 3</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 255</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Red, Green and Blue channels.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q30</span><div><p>In:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$(m,n,3)$$</div>\n<p>$m$ and $n$ represent:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Model and noise</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Image rows/height and columns/width</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Month and number</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Mean and normalization</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong></div></details></div></details></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4p-s19\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">36. Grayscale</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-widget\" data-vis=\"l4_rgb3d\"><div class=\"vis-title\">Images as matrices in 3D</div><div class=\"vis-root\"></div><figcaption>3D · An 8×8 image as RGB channel matrices, then grayscale, then a flattened vector.</figcaption></figure></div><p>The lecture gives approximately:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$Y=\n0.2125R+\n0.7154G+\n0.0721B$$</div>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q31</span><div><p>Converting RGB to grayscale changes approximately:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$(m,n,3)$$</div>\n<p>to:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> <div class=\"math-display\">$$(m,n)$$</div></div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> <div class=\"math-display\">$$(3,m,n,3)$$</div></div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> <div class=\"math-display\">$$1$$</div></div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> <div class=\"math-display\">$$(m^2,n^2)$$</div></div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q32</span><div><p>Why might grayscale be useful?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Reduces dimensionality while preserving intensity information</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Adds more colour channels</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Converts images into text</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Creates categories automatically</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong></div></details></div></details></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4p-s20\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">37. Flattening</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>A matrix:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">0.2 0.5\n0.7 0.9\n</code></pre>\n<p>can become:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">[0.2, 0.5, 0.7, 0.9]\n</code></pre>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q33</span><div><p>This is called:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Flattening</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Tokenization</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Binning</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Lemmatization</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q34</span><div><p>What is the main drawback?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Spatial neighbour relationships are largely lost</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Pixel values disappear</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Image size doubles</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> It creates extra colour channels</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong></div></details></div></details></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4p-s21\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">38. Intensity histograms</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q35</span><div><p>An image histogram can represent:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Number of pixels falling into different intensity ranges</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Word frequencies</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Time zones</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Categorical rankings</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q36</span><div><p>A mostly bright image is likely to have more pixel counts:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> At higher intensity values</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Only at zero</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> At negative intensity</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> At random text labels</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong></div></details></div></details></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4p-s22\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">39. Statistical image features</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q37</span><div><p>Which can summarize image intensity distributions?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Mean, variance, skewness, kurtosis</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Only SQL queries</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> TF-IDF only</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Generation labels</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong></div></details></div></details></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4p-s23\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">40. Canny edge detection</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q38</span><div><p>What does edge detection primarily seek?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Sharp changes in intensity/brightness</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Missing image labels</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Word counts</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Time zones</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q39</span><div><p>Which algorithm is specifically mentioned in the lecture?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Canny</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Apriori</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> PageRank</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> ARIMA</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong></div></details></div></details></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4p-s24\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">41. HOG</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q40</span><div><p>HOG stands for:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Histogram of Oriented Gradients</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Hierarchy of Global Groups</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> High Order Gaussian</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Hash of Gradients</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q41</span><div><p>HOG primarily captures:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Edge directions/orientations and local shape information</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Word semantics</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> SQL structure</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Datetime offsets</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong></div></details></div></details></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4p-s25\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">42. SURF</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q42</span><div><p>SURF is mainly associated with:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Localized feature extraction around interesting image points</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Text stemming</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Time binning</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Categorical encoding</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q43</span><div><p>SURF stands for:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Standard Unified Regression Features</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Speeded Up Robust Features</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Structured Universal Ranking Formula</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Scaled Unsupervised Random Forest</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong></div></details></div></details></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4p-s26\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">43. Visual Bag of Words</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q44</span><div><p>Visual Bag of Words is inspired by:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Text Bag of Words</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Min-Max scaling</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Regression</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Epoch timestamps</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q45</span><div><p>The basic VBOW flow is approximately:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Image → Local descriptors → Clusters → Visual words → Count vector\"><span>Image</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Local descriptors</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Clusters</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Visual words</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Count vector</span></div>\n<p>True or False?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> True</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> False</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>The lecture describes clustering local descriptors and treating clusters as visual words.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4p-s27\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">44. CNNs</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q46</span><div><p>What major change do CNNs bring compared with traditional image-feature engineering?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> CNNs can learn useful feature representations automatically</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> CNNs require all features to be manually designed</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> CNNs convert images to SQL</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> CNNs eliminate numerical input</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q47</span><div><p>Early CNN layers commonly learn:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Edges and lines</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Entire high-level objects immediately</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Time zones</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Document topics</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q48</span><div><p>Deeper CNN layers generally learn:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Increasingly complex patterns/concepts</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Only pixel brightness</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Missing-value indicators</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Stopwords</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong></div></details></div></details></div></details><h2 class=\"notes-divider\" id=\"l4p-d28\">PART D — INTEGRATION, CODING &amp; REVIEW</h2><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4p-s29\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">45. Lecture 4 coding questions</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><h4>Coding Q1 — Bag of Words</h4>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">from sklearn.feature_extraction.text import CountVectorizer\n\ncv = CountVectorizer()\nX = cv.fit_transform(documents)\n</code></pre>\n<p>What is <code>X</code>?</p>\n<p>A. Word-count feature representation<br />\nB. Timestamp matrix<br />\nC. Image matrix<br />\nD. Scaling parameters</p>\n<details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p><strong>Answer: A</strong></p></div></details>\n<hr />\n<h4>Coding Q2 — N-grams</h4>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">CountVectorizer(ngram_range=(1, 2))\n</code></pre>\n<p>What does this allow?</p>\n<p>A. Only single words<br />\nB. Unigrams and bigrams<br />\nC. Bigrams and trigrams only<br />\nD. Image patches</p>\n<details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p><strong>Answer: B</strong></p></div></details>\n<hr />\n<h4>Coding Q3 — TF-IDF</h4>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">from sklearn.feature_extraction.text import TfidfVectorizer\n\ntfidf = TfidfVectorizer()\nX = tfidf.fit_transform(documents)\n</code></pre>\n<p>What is being produced?</p>\n<p>A. Weighted text vectors<br />\nB. RGB images<br />\nC. Time intervals<br />\nD. One-hot countries</p>\n<details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p><strong>Answer: A</strong></p></div></details>\n<hr />\n<h4>Coding Q4 — cosine similarity</h4>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">from sklearn.metrics.pairwise import cosine_similarity\n\nsimilarity = cosine_similarity(X)\n</code></pre>\n<p>If two document vectors are nearly identical in direction, their similarity approaches:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$1$$</div>\n<hr />\n<h4>Coding Q5 — datetime features</h4>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">df[&quot;month&quot;] = df[&quot;time&quot;].dt.month\ndf[&quot;hour&quot;] = df[&quot;time&quot;].dt.hour\n</code></pre>\n<p>What is this?</p>\n<p>A. Temporal feature engineering<br />\nB. Text tokenization<br />\nC. Image normalization<br />\nD. Hashing</p>\n<details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p><strong>Answer: A</strong></p></div></details>\n<hr />\n<h4>Coding Q6 — time binning</h4>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">df[&quot;time_of_day&quot;] = pd.cut(\n    df[&quot;hour&quot;],\n    bins=[0, 6, 12, 18, 24],\n    labels=[&quot;Night&quot;, &quot;Morning&quot;, &quot;Afternoon&quot;, &quot;Evening&quot;]\n)\n</code></pre>\n<p>What technique is being combined?</p>\n<details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> Temporal extraction + binning.</p></div></details>\n<hr />\n<h4>Coding Q7 — image flattening</h4>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">X = image.flatten()\n</code></pre>\n<p>What happens?</p>\n<p>A. 2D/3D image data becomes a 1D vector<br />\nB. Text becomes lower case<br />\nC. RGB becomes TF-IDF<br />\nD. Image becomes categorical</p>\n<details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p><strong>Answer: A</strong></p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4p-s30\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">46. Integration / pipeline MCQs</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The lecture closes with the correct order:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Train/Test Split → Fit Transformers on Train → Transform Train → Transform Test → Train Model → Evaluate\"><span>Train/Test Split</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Fit Transformers on Train</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Transform Train</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Transform Test</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Train Model</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Evaluate</span></div>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q49</span><div><p>Why split train/test <strong>before</strong> fitting TF-IDF, quantile binning, scalers or encoders?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> To avoid test information leaking into preprocessing</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> To increase image resolution</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> To convert categories to text</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> To reduce rows automatically</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q50</span><div><p>Which is wrong?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Fit TF-IDF vocabulary on train, transform train/test</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Fit encoder on train, reuse on test</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Refit preprocessing separately on test</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Split before learning preprocessing parameters</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong></div></details></div></details></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4p-s31\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">47. Lecture 4 answer key</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Show the answer key</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th class=\"ta-r\">Q</th>\n<th class=\"ta-c\">Answer</th>\n<th class=\"ta-r\">Q</th>\n<th class=\"ta-c\">Answer</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">1</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">B</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">26</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">A</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">2</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">A</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">27</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">B</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">3</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">C</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">28</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">A</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">4</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">A</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">29</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">C</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">5</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">B</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">30</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">B</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">6</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">B</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">31</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">A</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">7</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">B</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">32</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">A</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">8</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">B</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">33</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">A</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">9</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">A</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">34</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">A</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">10</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">B</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">35</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">A</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">11</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">A</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">36</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">A</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">12</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">True</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">37</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">A</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">13</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">A</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">38</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">A</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">14</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">B</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">39</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">A</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">15</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">B</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">40</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">A</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">16</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">B</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">41</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">A</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">17</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">B</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">42</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">A</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">18</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">B</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">43</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">B</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">19</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">A</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">44</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">A</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">20</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">B</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">45</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">True</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">21</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">A</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">46</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">A</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">22</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">A</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">47</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">A</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">23</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">A</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">48</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">A</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">24</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">A</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">49</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">A</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">25</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">B</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">50</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">C</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<hr /></div></details></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4p-s32\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">48. The distinctions I would memorize hardest for Lecture 4</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>For <strong>text</strong>:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\n\\text{BoW}\n\\rightarrow\n\\text{Counts}\n}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\nN\\text{-}\\text{grams}\n\\rightarrow\n\\text{Some local word order}\n}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\n\\text{TFIDF}\n\\rightarrow\n\\text{Counts weighted by distinctiveness}\n}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\n\\text{Embeddings}\n\\rightarrow\n\\text{Semantic vectors}\n}$$</div>\n<p>Then:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{CBOW}:\\ \\text{Context}\\rightarrow \\text{Word}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{SkipGram}:\\ \\text{Word}\\rightarrow \\text{Context}$$</div>\n<p>For <strong>temporal</strong>:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\n\\text{Timestamp}\n\\rightarrow\n\\text{Year/Month/Day/Hour/Weekday/etc}.\n}$$</div>\n<p>and don't forget:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Normalize global timezones to}\\ a\\ \\text{common reference}}$$</div>\n<p>For <strong>images</strong>:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{RGB}=(\\text{height},\\text{width},3)}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\n\\text{Pixels}\n\\rightarrow\n\\text{Grayscale/Histogram/Edges/HOG/SURF}\n}$$</div>\n<p>or modern approach:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow boxed\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Pixels → CNN → Automatically learned features\"><span>Pixels</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>CNN</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Automatically learned features</span></div></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4p-s33\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">49. Final rapid-fire test for both lectures</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Answer each in about <strong>2–3 seconds</strong>:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Scenario</th>\n<th>Answer</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Only care whether count &gt; 0</td>\n<td>Binarization</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>87.638294 where decimals are noise</td>\n<td>Rounding</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Temperature × Humidity</td>\n<td>Interaction</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Age → 30–39</td>\n<td>Binning</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Same numerical range widths</td>\n<td>Fixed-width</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Same approximate observation counts</td>\n<td>Quantile</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Strong right-skewed salary</td>\n<td>Log transform</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Flexible power transform</td>\n<td>Box-Cox</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Colour</td>\n<td>Nominal</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Small &lt; Medium &lt; Large</td>\n<td>Ordinal</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Nominal text → binary columns</td>\n<td>One-hot</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>$m$ categories → $m-1$ columns</td>\n<td>Dummy coding</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Thousands of categories</td>\n<td>Feature hashing</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Split sentence into words</td>\n<td>Tokenization</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Count words</td>\n<td>BoW</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>\"not good\"</code> as one feature</td>\n<td>Bigram</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Penalize globally common terms</td>\n<td>TF-IDF</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Similarity ≈ 1</td>\n<td>Very similar vectors</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Discover topics</td>\n<td>LDA</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Semantic numerical word representations</td>\n<td>Embeddings</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Context → target word</td>\n<td>CBOW</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Target word → context</td>\n<td>Skip-gram</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Timestamp → month/hour</td>\n<td>Temporal feature extraction</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Malaysia and London timestamps</td>\n<td>Normalize UTC</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Seconds since 1970</td>\n<td>Epoch</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Image height × width × 3</td>\n<td>RGB</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>RGB → one intensity matrix</td>\n<td>Grayscale</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Detect sharp brightness changes</td>\n<td>Canny</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Edge orientation descriptor</td>\n<td>HOG</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Local keypoint descriptor</td>\n<td>SURF</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cluster local descriptors into visual words</td>\n<td>VBOW</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Automatically learn image features</td>\n<td>CNN</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>If you can do that table without hesitation, you have the high-yield conceptual material from <strong>Lectures 3 and 4</strong> in good shape.</p></div></details></div></details>");
